EXT. FROZEN ORCHARD OF GLASS APPLES - DUSK

A hillside of trees that grew crystal fruit and never stopped. Every apple holds a small captured flame. The snow around them glows amber.

WILHELMINA THRALE (70s, thick gray cloak, a ladder strapped to her back) climbs to the highest branch. She plucks one apple, careful as a surgeon, and drops it into her satchel. It does not clink. It purrs.

Below her, a boy waits. FINN CARRAWAY (11, bare ears, no gloves) holds the base of the ladder.

WILHELMINA
You've not eaten.

FINN
I'm fine.

WILHELMINA
There's bread in my left pocket.

Finn doesn't reach for it. Instead, he stares at the apples, his breath fogging.

FINN
They're getting dimmer.

WILHELMINA
They do, in winter.

FINN
Mam says they were bright all year, when Da was alive.

Wilhelmina lowers herself rung by rung. At the bottom she stands quietly beside him, close enough that her cloak brushes his shoulder.

WILHELMINA
Your Da climbed this ladder every night.

Finn picks at a thread on his sleeve.

FINN
Did he ever fall?

WILHELMINA
Twice. Never told your mother.

Finn almost smiles. He takes the bread from her pocket without asking and breaks it in two, offering her the bigger half.

She takes the smaller. He pretends not to notice.

An apple above them flares, bright as noon, then settles into a soft glow. They both look up.

FINN
Was that him?

Wilhelmina folds the cloak tighter around the boy's shoulders. She says nothing. She keeps her hand there.
